# vivijure-cf commit pin for quality-tier-drift only (local#295).
#
# WHY A PIN. local CI runs vivijure-core's full test suite, which includes
# tests/quality-tier-drift.test.ts. That test imports MANIFEST from
# vivijure-cf modules/keyframe and modules/own-gpu via a sibling checkout
# (see .github/workflows/ci.yml). Floating cf@main means an unrelated cf
# merge can red every open local PR with no signal on the cf side and no
# change in this repo -- the exact failure local#295 recorded.
#
# Bump this SHA deliberately when:
#   - core QUALITY_TIERS changes, or
#   - keyframe / own-gpu config_schema tier enums change on cf, or
#   - the module entrypoints gain imports that break the checkout (and you
#     have verified the pin still loads MANIFEST under core's vitest alias).
#
# NOT used by manifest-drift.yml (or containers-drift). Those jobs track
# cf@main on purpose: their job is to catch drift against current cf, not
# to freeze a quality-tier snapshot.
#
# STALENESS POLICY (local#295 follow-up). Nothing used to assert that this pin was still
# close to cf; .github/workflows/cf-pin-staleness.yml now does, on a SCHEDULE.
#
# It is deliberately NOT a pull_request check. Failing local PRs when cf drifts is the
# precise coupling this pin exists to remove, so asserting staleness there would rebuild
# the bug inside its own fix. A scheduled red blocks nobody and still gets noticed.
#
# Thresholds are generous on purpose: this catches a pin nobody has touched in a long
# time, it does not nag. Tighten either line if that turns out to be too loose.
MAX_BEHIND_COMMITS=150
MAX_AGE_DAYS=30
#
# Format: one SHA= line with a full 40-char lowercase hex commit.

